<p>A 16:9 video sits <em>in</em> a post. A 9:16 clip <strong>is</strong> the post on a phone — one viewport, the clip, its title and who made it. On a desk the same clip goes left and the post goes right. There are no like, share or "more" buttons anywhere: they belong to a feed of strangers, and this is a blog.</p>

<h2>The page</h2>
<p>Loaded here in an iframe, because that is the only honest way to show a layout whose whole claim is "it fits". Resize the frame and nothing ever scrolls.</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/short/" title="A vertical video page" style="height: 40rem" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}

<h2>How it fits</h2>
<p>The one thing that matters is sizing the player from <strong>both</strong> constraints at once:</p>
{{{{code "css"}}}}
.im-short {
	--im-short-gutter: clamp(0.75rem, 2vh, 2rem);
	--im-short-room: calc(100dvh - var(--im-bar-height) - var(--im-short-gutter) * 2);

	height: calc(100dvh - var(--im-bar-height));
	display: grid;
	place-items: center;
	overflow: hidden;
}

.im-short-player {
	width: min(100%, calc(var(--im-short-room) * 9 / 16));
	aspect-ratio: 9 / 16;
}
{{{{/code}}}}
<p>The clip is as tall as the room allows <em>until</em> the room is narrower than that, and then it is as wide as the room allows. <code>aspect-ratio</code> supplies the other dimension either way. Nothing overflows, so there is nothing for a scrollbar to appear about.</p>
<ul>
	<li><strong>A height in <code>vh</code> with <code>overflow: hidden</code> is not the same thing.</strong> That hides the overflow rather than preventing it: the controls end up off the bottom of the screen on a laptop, and nobody can reach them.</li>
	<li><strong><code>100dvh</code>, not <code>100vh</code>.</strong> On a phone the browser's own bars come and go, and <code>vh</code> is the tallest the viewport ever gets — which is the one number that is wrong while those bars are showing.</li>
	<li><strong>The gutter is in <code>vh</code> too</strong> (<code>clamp(0.75rem, 2vh, 2rem)</code>), so a short window gives up its padding before it gives up the picture.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Parts</h2>
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>Class</th><th></th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td><code>im-short</code></td><td>The stage: one viewport, centred, clipped. <code>im-short-full</code> when there is no top bar above it.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-short-player</code></td><td>The 9:16 frame. Takes an <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>, a <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> or an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-short-over</code></td><td>What is written over the clip — channel, title, tags — on a gradient scrim, so the picture survives.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-short-bar</code></td><td>The scrubber, on the clip's bottom edge.</td></tr>
		<tr><td><code>im-short-step</code></td><td>Up and down — the whole of a short feed's navigation. Hidden on a phone, where the gesture is the swipe.</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>In a theme</h2>
{{{{code "hbs"}}}}
{{!-- short.hbs — a post tagged #short, with the vertical clip --}}
{{#post}}
<div class="im-short im-short-full">
	<div class="im-short-stage">
		<div class="im-short-player">
			<video src="{{@custom.clip}}" poster="{{img_url feature_image}}" muted loop playsinline autoplay></video>
			<div class="im-short-over"> … </div>
			<span class="im-short-bar"><span></span></span>
		</div>
		<div class="im-short-rail"> … </div>
	</div>
</div>
{{/post}}
{{{{/code}}}}
<p>Give it no footer and no sidebar: a page that is one viewport has no room for either, and a footer that can only be reached by scrolling defeats the whole layout. If the site needs those, the short belongs in a <a href="/collections/video/">collection</a> and this page belongs behind a link from it.</p>

<h2>The other two 9:16 layouts</h2>
<p>One clip is a page; several are a <strong>feed</strong>; and on a desk, where there is room beside the clip, it is a <strong>watch page</strong> — the same viewport, split.</p>

<h3>The watch page: clip left, everything else right</h3>
<p>A 9:16 clip on a 16:9 screen leaves two thirds of the window empty. <code>im-shortwatch</code> puts the <em>post</em> there — title, byline, the body, the facts, one comment — and the panel owns the only scrollbar on the page.</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/shortwatch/" title="A vertical video with its details beside it" style="height: 40rem" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}
<ul>
	<li><strong>Same two-constraint sizing as everything else here</strong> — <code>width: min(100%, room * 9/16)</code> with <code>aspect-ratio</code>, so the clip is as tall as the room allows until the room is narrower than that.</li>
	<li><strong>Previous and next are a column beside the clip</strong>, not buttons floating over it — and they are the only controls. No reactions.</li>
	<li><strong>Under 64rem it stops pretending.</strong> There is no room for two columns, so it becomes an ordinary page — clip, then panel, window scrolls. A layout that insists on fitting when it cannot is how controls end up off-screen.</li>
	<li><strong>The comments are <a href="/collections/series/episode/">lean</a></strong>: the box and one reply. The viewer is here for the clip.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The feed: one column that snaps</h3>
<p>One short is a page. Several are a <strong>feed</strong> — the same page repeated down a snapping column, where the scroll <em>is</em> the navigation. Scroll inside the frame.</p>
{{#example flush=true code=false}}
<iframe class="demo-frame" src="/demos/shorts/" title="A feed of vertical videos" style="height: 40rem" loading="lazy"></iframe>
{{/example}}
{{{{code "css"}}}}
.im-shortfeed {
	height: 100dvh;
	overflow-y: auto;
	scroll-snap-type: y mandatory;
	overscroll-behavior-y: contain;
}

.im-shortfeed > .im-short {
	height: 100%;
	scroll-snap-align: start;
	scroll-snap-stop: always;
}
{{{{/code}}}}
<ul>
	<li><strong><code>scroll-snap-stop: always</code> is the whole feel of it.</strong> Without it a hard flick skips three clips, which is the one thing a feed must never do — a viewer who cannot land on the clip they aimed at stops aiming.</li>
	<li><strong><code>overscroll-behavior-y: contain</code></strong> stops the last clip handing the scroll back to the page behind it.</li>
	<li><strong>No next button.</strong> The gesture is the button; <code>im-short-step</code> belongs on the single page, where there is nothing to scroll.</li>
	<li><strong>Only the first poster is eager.</strong> Four autoplaying videos is four videos downloading at once — in a real feed, play the snapped one and pause the rest with one <code>IntersectionObserver</code> on the column.</li>
	<li><strong>Each clip is a landmark</strong> (<code>&lt;section aria-label="Short 2 of 4"&gt;</code>), so a screen-reader user knows where they are in a list that has no visible list.</li>
	<li><code>im-shortfeed-inset</code> when there is a top bar above the column instead of it filling the window.</li>
</ul>

<h2>In a grid</h2>
<p>A vertical card is the same <a href="/collections/video/card/">video card</a> with one variable changed — <code>im-vidcard-tall</code> sets <code>--im-vidcard-ratio: 9 / 16</code>. Not a second component.</p>
{{#example}}
<div class="im-vidgrid" style="--im-vidgrid-min: 11rem">
	{{#each demo.videos}}{{#match @index "<" 5}}{{> "components/video-card" class="im-vidcard-tall" hide_by=true}}{{/match}}{{/each}}
</div>
{{/example}}

<h2>Details that matter</h2>
<ul>
	<li><strong>Autoplay muted, looping, <code>playsinline</code>.</strong> Anything else is blocked, and <code>playsinline</code> is what stops iOS taking the clip full-screen out from under the layout.</li>
	<li><strong>Do not hide the title behind a tap.</strong> It is the only thing telling a viewer what they are looking at.</li>
	<li><strong>One viewport means one viewport.</strong> If the design needs a description, comments and related clips, it is not a short page — it is a <a href="/collections/video/page/">video page</a> with a vertical player.</li>
</ul>
